  "geometry_rationale": {
    "pelvis": "Right-minus-left ASIS gives lateral; ASIS-midpoint minus PSIS-midpoint projected perpendicular to lateral gives anterior; lateral cross anterior gives superior. This uses identified anatomical landmarks rather than assuming global +Z signifies flexion.",
    "hip": "Femur origin is hip child-joint location zero in bound XML; vector to femur medial/lateral knee marker midpoint, tilt toward pelvis anterior is diagnostic flexion.",
    "knee": "Tibia origin is moving knee child-joint location zero. Vector to tibia malleolus midpoint vs thigh vector defines relative sagittal bend. Native realization retains knee coordinate-dependent SimmSpline translations; no fixed-hinge reconstruction.",
    "ankle": "Heel marker to mean first/fifth metatarsal markers defines foot direction. Increasing foot elevation relative to shank tilt is diagnostic dorsiflexion. Subtalar/MTP are held at zero; ankle oblique axis retained natively.",
    "limits": "These landmark angles test local direction, not equality to source generalized angle magnitude, an ISB angle decomposition, clinical neutral offsets, or canonical segment Euler angles. Marker placement errors may affect diagnostics and must be reviewed on actual probe."
  },
